1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:01,302
9:20 PM,

2
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:04,271
November 26, 2008.

3
00:00:05,547 --> 00:00:08,047
Ten men step off inflatable speedboats

4
00:00:08,073 --> 00:00:09,623
onto the Mumbai shoreline.

5
00:00:09,998 --> 00:00:11,209
Each has a rifle.

6
00:00:11,587 --> 00:00:12,602
Each has a phone.

7
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:16,322
In the next ten minutes, five attacks

8
00:00:16,335 --> 00:00:17,728
begin across the city.

9
00:00:18,145 --> 00:00:20,827
Over the next sixty hours, this becomes

10
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:23,249
the longest hostage siege India has ever

11
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:23,809
faced.

12
00:00:25,082 --> 00:00:27,048
But here is the part that still doesn't

13
00:00:27,061 --> 00:00:27,491
fit.

14
00:00:27,908 --> 00:00:29,913
The men pulling the triggers weren't the

15
00:00:29,926 --> 00:00:31,033
ones in charge.

16
00:00:31,423 --> 00:00:33,546
The orders came down a phone line, from a

17
00:00:33,559 --> 00:00:36,124
room six hundred kilometers away — where

18
00:00:36,137 --> 00:00:38,181
someone was watching the siege on live

19
00:00:38,194 --> 00:00:38,871
television.

20
00:00:40,012 --> 00:00:42,538
So who was really holding Mumbai hostage?

21
00:00:49,957 --> 00:00:51,715
Everything that follows comes from the

22
00:00:51,728 --> 00:00:54,397
record — police and NSG timelines, court

23
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:57,014
testimony, and the recordings themselves.

24
00:00:57,418 --> 00:00:59,306
And the record says this story doesn't

25
00:00:59,319 --> 00:01:01,259
start on November 26th.

26
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:04,284
It starts five days earlier.

27
00:01:05,473 --> 00:01:07,296
On the night of November 21st,

28
00:01:07,804 --> 00:01:10,369
ten men slipped out of Karachi aboard two

29
00:01:10,382 --> 00:01:11,320
cargo vessels,

30
00:01:11,918 --> 00:01:13,924
the Al Fauz and the Al Hussaini.

31
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:16,593
For roughly thirty-eight hours, the Indian

32
00:01:16,606 --> 00:01:17,869
Navy never saw them.

33
00:01:18,286 --> 00:01:21,072
Each man carried an AK-47 and eight

34
00:01:21,085 --> 00:01:21,749
grenades.

35
00:01:22,125 --> 00:01:24,130
And dried fruit, for the journey.

36
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:26,614
On November 23rd,

37
00:01:27,213 --> 00:01:28,841
at around 4:30 PM,

38
00:01:29,427 --> 00:01:31,432
they seized an Indian fishing trawler,

39
00:01:31,927 --> 00:01:33,177
the MV Kuber.

40
00:01:33,567 --> 00:01:35,169
They killed four of its crew.

41
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:37,604
The captain, they kept alive — to sail

42
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:38,502
them to India.

43
00:01:39,794 --> 00:01:41,799
Within sight of Mumbai, they killed him

44
00:01:41,812 --> 00:01:42,203
too.

45
00:01:42,607 --> 00:01:44,911
No one was left who could warn anyone.

46
00:01:46,097 --> 00:01:48,493
Except the warnings already existed.

47
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:50,993
Indian intelligence had intercepted a

48
00:01:51,006 --> 00:01:53,688
satellite call on November 18th about a

49
00:01:53,701 --> 00:01:54,795
sea-borne attack.

50
00:01:55,171 --> 00:01:57,918
US intelligence had warned the Taj Hotels

51
00:01:57,931 --> 00:02:01,147
chairman, Ratan Tata, months before.

52
00:02:02,412 --> 00:02:03,232
Two warnings.

53
00:02:03,623 --> 00:02:05,798
Neither one changed what was waiting on

54
00:02:05,811 --> 00:02:06,410
the shore.

55
00:02:07,791 --> 00:02:09,887
They came ashore between 8:00  and

56
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:10,942
8:30 PM.

57
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:14,335
Two landing points on Cuffe Parade:

58
00:02:14,674 --> 00:02:17,161
Macchimar Nagar and Badhwar Park.

59
00:02:17,567 --> 00:02:19,793
When curious locals asked who they were,

60
00:02:20,158 --> 00:02:21,473
they said they were students.

61
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:24,452
Then they split into pairs.

62
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:27,935
Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus.

63
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:30,749
One of the busiest railway stations on

64
00:02:30,762 --> 00:02:31,283
earth.

65
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:35,032
Ajmal Kasab and his partner, Ismail Khan,

66
00:02:35,331 --> 00:02:37,727
walked into the passenger hall and opened

67
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:38,261
fire.

68
00:02:38,653 --> 00:02:40,775
They threw grenades into the crowd.

69
00:02:41,192 --> 00:02:42,976
Fifty-eight people were killed here.

70
00:02:44,234 --> 00:02:46,382
It was the deadliest single site of the

71
00:02:46,395 --> 00:02:47,489
entire attack.

72
00:02:48,658 --> 00:02:51,223
Leopold Café, a few minutes south.

73
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:53,723
Two gunmen sprayed it with bullets and

74
00:02:53,736 --> 00:02:54,830
killed ten people.

75
00:02:55,221 --> 00:02:56,718
Then they fled on foot.

76
00:02:57,109 --> 00:02:59,413
The explosives they'd left in two taxis

77
00:02:59,426 --> 00:03:00,507
killed five more.

78
00:03:01,683 --> 00:03:03,259
And at almost the same moment,

79
00:03:03,766 --> 00:03:05,394
pairs of gunmen walked into the

80
00:03:05,394 --> 00:03:08,050
Taj Mahal Palace and the Oberoi-Trident.

81
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:10,785
Two five-star hotels that would become the

82
00:03:10,798 --> 00:03:12,503
main stage for three days.

83
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,936
The fifth target was one most of the world

84
00:03:15,949 --> 00:03:17,042
had never heard of:

85
00:03:17,537 --> 00:03:19,529
a small Jewish outreach centre called

86
00:03:19,529 --> 00:03:20,584
Nariman House.

87
00:03:21,759 --> 00:03:22,931
Five locations.

88
00:03:23,322 --> 00:03:24,116
Ten minutes.

89
00:03:24,492 --> 00:03:25,195
Ten men.

90
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:28,450
This is what eighteen months of rehearsal

91
00:03:28,463 --> 00:03:30,898
looks like — the curriculum from our last

92
00:03:30,911 --> 00:03:32,304
file, finally deployed.

93
00:03:32,708 --> 00:03:35,104
But rehearsal alone doesn't explain what

94
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:36,081
happened next.

95
00:03:37,478 --> 00:03:39,392
The gunmen weren't improvising.

96
00:03:40,654 --> 00:03:42,021
They were being directed.

97
00:03:43,252 --> 00:03:45,726
Intercepted wireless communications show

98
00:03:45,739 --> 00:03:47,940
them taking real-time instructions from a

99
00:03:47,953 --> 00:03:49,763
control room in Pakistan.

100
00:03:50,166 --> 00:03:52,224
That room was watching Indian television

101
00:03:52,237 --> 00:03:54,281
news — tracking troop movements and

102
00:03:54,294 --> 00:03:55,427
hostage positions,

103
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,005
then relaying it back to the men inside

104
00:03:58,018 --> 00:03:58,747
the hotels.

105
00:03:59,151 --> 00:04:01,000
The siege was being broadcast.

106
00:04:01,429 --> 00:04:03,721
And the broadcast was being used as

107
00:04:03,734 --> 00:04:04,606
intelligence.

108
00:04:05,023 --> 00:04:07,497
When Indian authorities realised it, they

109
00:04:07,510 --> 00:04:09,867
cut the television feeds to the hotels.

110
00:04:11,059 --> 00:04:13,364
David Headley later testified that he

111
00:04:13,377 --> 00:04:15,564
recognised the voices in that room.

112
00:04:15,981 --> 00:04:18,494
His own handler, Sajid Mir.

113
00:04:19,738 --> 00:04:22,811
And two others, known as Abu Khafa and

114
00:04:22,824 --> 00:04:23,918
Abu Al Kama.

115
00:04:24,336 --> 00:04:26,185
Recordings played in court reportedly

116
00:04:26,198 --> 00:04:27,904
captured the control room telling the

117
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:30,417
gunmen that Mumbai's anti-terrorism chief

118
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:31,394
had just been killed.

119
00:04:31,769 --> 00:04:34,334
News they heard from Karachi — about

120
00:04:34,347 --> 00:04:36,743
something happening a few kilometres away,

121
00:04:37,108 --> 00:04:38,866
outside Cama Hospital.

122
00:04:40,216 --> 00:04:43,497
From CST, Kasab and Ismail Khan

123
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:45,698
moved on foot to Cama Hospital.

124
00:04:46,117 --> 00:04:48,057
It was around 10:30 PM.

125
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:49,945
The staff saw them coming.

126
00:04:50,335 --> 00:04:52,484
They locked patients into their rooms.

127
00:04:53,764 --> 00:04:55,952
That decision likely saved lives.

128
00:04:57,128 --> 00:04:59,823
Outside, three of Mumbai's most senior

129
00:04:59,836 --> 00:05:01,777
officers tried to stop them from a jeep:

130
00:05:02,623 --> 00:05:05,383
Anti-Terrorism Squad chief Hemant Karkare,

131
00:05:06,008 --> 00:05:08,391
additional commissioner Ashok Kamte, and

132
00:05:08,404 --> 00:05:10,474
senior inspector Vijay Salaskar.

133
00:05:10,865 --> 00:05:12,089
All three were killed.

134
00:05:12,493 --> 00:05:14,758
The gunmen took the same police jeep and

135
00:05:14,771 --> 00:05:15,553
drove off.

136
00:05:15,943 --> 00:05:17,011
They didn't get far.

137
00:05:17,387 --> 00:05:20,382
Near Girgaum Chowpatty, officers from the

138
00:05:20,395 --> 00:05:22,921
Gamdevi police station stopped the jeep.

139
00:05:23,298 --> 00:05:25,147
Ismail Khan was killed.

140
00:05:26,389 --> 00:05:29,618
Kasab, wounded, was taken alive.

141
00:05:30,814 --> 00:05:32,963
He was the only one of the ten who would

142
00:05:32,976 --> 00:05:33,327
be.

143
00:05:33,744 --> 00:05:35,840
And that one survivor is the reason the

144
00:05:35,853 --> 00:05:38,549
world would later hear, in his own words,

145
00:05:38,757 --> 00:05:40,358
how the operation was built.

146
00:05:40,762 --> 00:05:43,431
By 1:00 AM, this thread of the night was

147
00:05:43,444 --> 00:05:43,887
over.

148
00:05:45,135 --> 00:05:47,349
The other four were just getting started.

149
00:05:48,757 --> 00:05:51,166
Four gunmen had entered the Taj at around

150
00:05:51,179 --> 00:05:52,755
9:44 PM.

151
00:05:53,145 --> 00:05:55,202
They moved through the lobby and up toward

152
00:05:55,216 --> 00:05:56,309
the grand staircase.

153
00:05:56,726 --> 00:05:58,588
According to a West Point case study of

154
00:05:58,601 --> 00:06:01,192
the siege, it was a position that let them

155
00:06:01,205 --> 00:06:03,015
watch the main entrance and everything

156
00:06:03,028 --> 00:06:03,601
below it.

157
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:06,534
They had chosen the high ground.

158
00:06:07,705 --> 00:06:08,799
By 11:00 PM,

159
00:06:09,346 --> 00:06:11,130
police had surrounded the building.

160
00:06:11,533 --> 00:06:13,018
Then, at 1:00 AM,

161
00:06:13,617 --> 00:06:15,609
a blast tore through the central dome.

162
00:06:16,025 --> 00:06:18,213
Fire spread through the heritage wing.

163
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:21,130
Army units arrived by 2:30 AM,

164
00:06:21,690 --> 00:06:23,447
and fire engines by three.

165
00:06:23,864 --> 00:06:24,567
Through the dark,

166
00:06:25,062 --> 00:06:27,158
firefighters raised ladders and pulled

167
00:06:27,171 --> 00:06:29,190
more than two hundred guests to safety

168
00:06:29,593 --> 00:06:31,598
while gun battles went on inside.

169
00:06:32,789 --> 00:06:35,068
By dawn, the fire was largely under

170
00:06:35,081 --> 00:06:35,693
control.

171
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:38,132
The siege was not.

172
00:06:38,562 --> 00:06:40,528
The gunmen had moved into the newer

173
00:06:40,541 --> 00:06:42,911
tower — holding somewhere between one

174
00:06:42,924 --> 00:06:44,096
hundred and one hundred and fifty

175
00:06:44,109 --> 00:06:44,838
hostages.

176
00:06:46,257 --> 00:06:49,135
On the morning of the 27th came a decision

177
00:06:49,148 --> 00:06:51,361
that has been criticised ever since.

178
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:54,161
Local police were told to stand down and

179
00:06:54,174 --> 00:06:56,218
hand full authority to the National

180
00:06:56,231 --> 00:06:57,012
Security Guard —

181
00:06:57,702 --> 00:06:59,968
India's federal counter-terrorism force.

182
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:03,765
Its commandos had to fly in from Delhi.

183
00:07:05,015 --> 00:07:06,044
The city waited.

184
00:07:07,229 --> 00:07:10,719
At the Taj, NSG commandos were inside by

185
00:07:10,732 --> 00:07:12,568
around 7:20 that evening.

186
00:07:12,984 --> 00:07:13,661
Until then,

187
00:07:14,143 --> 00:07:15,615
the fighting came in bursts:

188
00:07:16,357 --> 00:07:18,948
small evacuations, sudden gunfire,

189
00:07:19,573 --> 00:07:21,201
a fire on the fourth floor.

190
00:07:22,377 --> 00:07:23,875
At the Oberoi-Trident,

191
00:07:24,434 --> 00:07:27,572
the NSG arrived around 5:30 PM.

192
00:07:27,989 --> 00:07:30,450
A naval helicopter surveyed the building

193
00:07:30,463 --> 00:07:31,270
from above.

194
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:34,513
Roughly 250 guests would be brought out of

195
00:07:34,526 --> 00:07:35,698
this hotel alone,

196
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:37,612
in waves through the night.

197
00:07:38,756 --> 00:07:41,542
At Nariman House, there was nothing to

198
00:07:41,556 --> 00:07:42,415
negotiate.

199
00:07:42,834 --> 00:07:45,282
Two gunmen held the rabbi, his wife, and

200
00:07:45,295 --> 00:07:46,271
several others.

201
00:07:46,662 --> 00:07:49,123
The NSG would not move in until the

202
00:07:49,136 --> 00:07:50,086
following morning.

203
00:07:51,268 --> 00:07:53,573
By the end of the second day, none of the

204
00:07:53,586 --> 00:07:55,109
three sieges was over.

205
00:07:56,375 --> 00:07:58,875
Twenty-four hours, and no end in sight.

206
00:08:00,244 --> 00:08:03,031
The 28th is when two of the sieges finally

207
00:08:03,044 --> 00:08:03,447
end.

208
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:06,904
Neither ends the way anyone hoped.

209
00:08:08,093 --> 00:08:10,945
At Nariman House, just after 7:30 in the

210
00:08:10,958 --> 00:08:11,426
morning,

211
00:08:11,999 --> 00:08:14,760
NSG commandos fast-roped from helicopters

212
00:08:14,773 --> 00:08:17,234
onto the roof, with snipers covering them

213
00:08:17,247 --> 00:08:18,510
from nearby buildings.

214
00:08:18,926 --> 00:08:20,229
It took most of the day.

215
00:08:20,671 --> 00:08:23,184
By 7:30 in the evening, they had found all

216
00:08:23,197 --> 00:08:25,828
six hostages — the rabbi and his wife

217
00:08:25,841 --> 00:08:28,341
among them — murdered by their captors.

218
00:08:28,718 --> 00:08:31,205
An hour later, both gunmen were dead,

219
00:08:31,674 --> 00:08:33,562
and the siege was declared over.

220
00:08:34,737 --> 00:08:37,706
At the Oberoi-Trident, the operation ended

221
00:08:37,719 --> 00:08:38,995
around 3:00 PM.

222
00:08:39,386 --> 00:08:41,143
Officials counted one hundred and

223
00:08:41,156 --> 00:08:43,604
forty-three hostages rescued alive.

224
00:08:44,008 --> 00:08:46,104
Twenty-four bodies were recovered.

225
00:08:46,521 --> 00:08:48,591
Both terrorists inside were killed.

226
00:08:49,752 --> 00:08:51,601
The Taj, though, was still burning.

227
00:08:52,018 --> 00:08:53,880
Six more bodies were recovered that

228
00:08:53,893 --> 00:08:54,505
afternoon,

229
00:08:55,130 --> 00:08:57,395
followed by ten grenade blasts between

230
00:08:57,408 --> 00:09:00,429
2:53 and 3:59 PM.

231
00:09:00,846 --> 00:09:01,510
By evening,

232
00:09:02,057 --> 00:09:04,335
officials believed only one gunman was

233
00:09:04,348 --> 00:09:05,325
left inside.

234
00:09:06,571 --> 00:09:07,391
They were wrong.

235
00:09:09,404 --> 00:09:12,360
In the early hours of the 29th, the Taj

236
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:13,102
shook again.

237
00:09:13,519 --> 00:09:14,743
Five explosions,

238
00:09:15,238 --> 00:09:17,985
between 3:40 and 4:10 AM.

239
00:09:18,402 --> 00:09:19,339
Ten minutes later,

240
00:09:19,977 --> 00:09:21,995
officials declared the hotel under

241
00:09:22,008 --> 00:09:23,063
government control.

242
00:09:24,338 --> 00:09:26,669
By 5:05, they had taken it back.

243
00:09:27,835 --> 00:09:29,176
At 7:30 AM,

244
00:09:29,788 --> 00:09:32,236
fire was still burning on the first floor.

245
00:09:32,653 --> 00:09:34,710
Black smoke rose from the second.

246
00:09:35,127 --> 00:09:37,640
Witnesses described a gun battle still

247
00:09:37,653 --> 00:09:38,330
under way.

248
00:09:38,747 --> 00:09:40,895
Then, sometime between 8:00  and

249
00:09:40,908 --> 00:09:41,937
8:50 AM  —

250
00:09:42,445 --> 00:09:44,931
accounts differ by minutes — commandos

251
00:09:44,945 --> 00:09:47,861
declared the hotel finally, fully secured.

252
00:09:48,252 --> 00:09:50,973
The NSG called it Operation Tornado.

253
00:09:52,151 --> 00:09:54,625
From the first shots at CST to the last

254
00:09:54,638 --> 00:09:57,242
all-clear at the Taj, the siege had run

255
00:09:57,255 --> 00:09:58,701
just under sixty hours.

256
00:10:00,078 --> 00:10:02,917
By the official count, 166 people were

257
00:10:02,930 --> 00:10:03,490
killed.

258
00:10:03,919 --> 00:10:05,951
More than a dozen of them were security

259
00:10:05,964 --> 00:10:08,906
personnel, and at least 26 were foreign

260
00:10:08,919 --> 00:10:09,635
nationals.

261
00:10:10,052 --> 00:10:12,148
More than 300 were injured.

262
00:10:13,393 --> 00:10:15,385
Nine of the ten attackers died.

263
00:10:15,802 --> 00:10:18,536
Only Ajmal Kasab survived — tried,

264
00:10:19,031 --> 00:10:21,909
convicted, and executed in 2012.

265
00:10:23,095 --> 00:10:26,168
On 12 February 2009, Pakistan's

266
00:10:26,181 --> 00:10:27,809
government admitted something for the

267
00:10:27,822 --> 00:10:28,538
first time.

268
00:10:29,797 --> 00:10:31,841
The attack had been planned and launched,

269
00:10:31,867 --> 00:10:34,198
at least in part, from Pakistani soil.

270
00:10:34,574 --> 00:10:37,204
It continued to deny any official state

271
00:10:37,217 --> 00:10:37,959
involvement.

272
00:10:38,337 --> 00:10:41,553
It denied, specifically, any role for its

273
00:10:41,566 --> 00:10:44,118
intelligence services — a role multiple

274
00:10:44,131 --> 00:10:46,071
foreign investigators have alleged.

275
00:10:47,238 --> 00:10:49,152
The city didn't wait for answers.

276
00:10:49,529 --> 00:10:52,198
CST was running again within hours.

277
00:10:52,603 --> 00:10:55,025
Leopold Café was reportedly serving

278
00:10:55,038 --> 00:10:56,834
customers within four days.

279
00:10:58,094 --> 00:11:00,516
The Taj reopened to guests within about a

280
